79:Some families are segment-preserving rather than all-or-nothing: sequence keeps `alt`/`opt`/`loop`/`par`/`critical`/`break`/`rect` as typed fragments (with their own read-back and mutation ops, and their `create`/`destroy` lines, notes, activation lines and nested fragments, which every fragment op reaches through an optional `fragmentPath` of `{ fragmentIndex, branchIndex }` steps, and which `move_fragment` moves between branches and the top level with `toFragmentPath`) and top-level `create`/`destroy`, notes and `activate`/`deactivate` lines as typed statements (edited, with message `+`/`-` marks, by `add_activation`, `remove_activation` and `set_message_activation`) while box/autonumber/title and comments ride along verbatim (at top level, or inside a fragment branch beside its typed statements; a kept branch line never holds a message or note), and its top-level message indices stay separate from fragment-message indices (facts state each message's op address as `message#N op [fragmentPath P] [fragmentIndex F branchIndex B] index K`, and each activation line's address and each activation span by the messages around it); a typed edit that would leave a `create`/`destroy` apart from its message, or a deactivation of a participant that is not active, is refused rather than written or balanced by dropping authored lines; gantt keeps calendar directives and click/comment lines as verbatim segments; ER keeps ordered typed and opaque-block segments. Only input that cannot be segmented (e.g. an unbalanced `end`) falls back to a whole-body opaque body.
